<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ramon Berenguer IV, Count of Provence</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1198">1198</a>–1245</span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Raymond Berengar IV of Provence · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-barcelona">House of Barcelona</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ramon Berenguer IV was Count of Provence and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/forcalquier">Forcalquier</a> from 1209 until 1245, the last of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barcelona">Barcelona</a> line in Provence. He strengthened comital government and married his four daughters into royal houses: Margaret to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-ix-of-france">Louis IX of France</a>, Eleanor to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-iii-of-england">Henry III of England</a>, Sanchia to Richard of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cornwall">Cornwall</a> and Beatrice, his heiress, to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-i-of-anjou">Charles of Anjou</a>. Through Beatrice, Provence passed to the Angevins in 1246.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ramon-berenguer-iv-count-of-provence.jpg" alt="Ramon Berenguer IV, Count of Provence" width="320" height="309" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ramon Berenguer V de Provença (1209-1245) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Segell-ramon-berenguer-V-proven%C3%A7a_(1209-1245).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Ramon Berenguer was the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-ii-count-of-provence">Alfonso II, Count of Provence</a>, and Garsenda, Countess of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/forcalquier">Forcalquier</a>. After his father's death in 1209 his mother sent him to the Templar castle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monzon">Monzón</a> in Aragon, in the company of his cousin James, whose life was also under threat. He left Monzón in 1216 to claim his inheritance, which included Forcalquier from his mother. He was the first count of Provence in over a hundred years to live in the county. On 5 June 1219 he married Beatrice of Savoy, a daughter of Thomas, Count of Savoy, who gave the 21-year-old count a powerful father-in-law to protect his interests.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Ramon Berenguer and his wife supported troubadours, and he composed Provençal verse himself. He wrote laws forbidding nobles from doing menial work such as farming. He fought border disputes with the counts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toulouse">Toulouse</a>, and in 1226 began to reassert his rights in Marseille, whose citizens turned first to Thomas of Savoy and then to Raymond VII of Toulouse. In 1228 he supported Thomas in a war against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turin">Turin</a> and Guigues VI of Viennois, over control of the trade routes from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>.</p><p>In 1233 Blanche of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> sent a knight to Provence, and she then arranged the marriage of her son Louis to his eldest daughter Margaret, with a dowry of ten thousand <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> marks, for which the count had to pledge castles. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-iii-of-england">Henry III of England</a> then sought Eleanor's hand, and the count negotiated that no dowry be paid, promising her ten thousand marks in his will.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 1238 Ramon Berenguer joined his brother-in-law Amadeus IV at the court of Emperor Frederick II in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turin">Turin</a>. In January 1244 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-innocent-iv">Pope Innocent IV</a> decreed that only the pope could excommunicate him, and in 1245 he sent representatives to the First Council of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lyon">Lyon</a>. He died at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aix-en-provence">Aix-en-Provence</a> in August 1245 and left the county to his youngest daughter Beatrice. At least two planhs, Occitan funeral laments, were written in his honour. The chronicler Giovanni Villani called him a wise and courteous lord whose court drew gentle persons from Provence, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and Catalonia.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ramon Berenguer V of Provence” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Their son <b>Raymond Berengar IV</b> ruled both.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-forcalquier">County of Forcalquier</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Raymond Berengar IV</b>'s daughter Beatrice married Charles of Anjou in 1246, and the Angevin counts, also kings of Naples, ruled from Aix and Naples.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-provence">County of Provence</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aix-en-provence">Aix-en-Provence</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aix-en-provence">Aix-en-Provence</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=43.53,5.45&amp;year=1228" aria-label="Aix-en-Provence on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Ramon Berenguer IV, Count of Provence"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5337 3846h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5337 3846h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=43.53,5.45&amp;year=1228">What was here in 1228? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
